      "text": "To determine how many students did not receive an A in either math or history, we can use the principle of inclusion and exclusion. Let's define the following:\n\n- Let \\( H \\) be the set of students who received an A in history.\n- Let \\( M \\) be the set of students who received an A in math.\n- The total number of students is 30.\n\nFrom the problem, we know:\n- \\( |H| = 7 \\) (the number of students who received an A in history).\n- \\( |M| = 13 \\) (the number of students who received an A in math).\n- \\( |H \\cap M| = 4 \\) (the number of students who received an A in both history and math).\n\nWe need to find the number of students who received an A in either history or math or both. This can be calculated using the principle of inclusion and exclusion:\n\n\\[\n|H \\cup M| = |H| + |M| - |H \\cap M|\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the given values:\n\n\\[\n|H \\cup M| = 7 + 13 - 4 = 16\n\\]\n\nSo, 16 students received an A in either history or math or both. To find the number of students who did not receive an A in either course, we subtract the number of students who received an A in at least one of the courses from the total number of students:\n\n\\[\n30 - |H \\cup M| = 30 - 16 = 14\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the number of students who did not receive an A in any of these two courses is \\(\\boxed{14}\\).",
